No real-mode trampoline for a cooperative guest.

reserve_real_mode() allocates a page below 1 MB for the code an AP executes on
its way from real mode into long mode, and for the stub the machine returns
through when resuming from ACPI sleep. A cooperative guest has neither. There
are no APs to start -- the host owns the processors and hands over one thread
at a time -- and there is no firmware underneath to sleep into.

It is also memory this guest cannot supply. Its physical memory is whatever
contiguous blocks the host could spare, described at their true host physical
addresses, and none of them is below 1 MB. So the allocation fails, and the
kernel says so:

    No sub-1M memory is available for the trampoline

which is accurate and harmless on its own. What is not harmless is what comes
later: init_real_mode() opens with

    if (!real_mode_header)
            panic("Real mode trampoline was not allocated");

and it runs as an early_initcall, so a guest that gets that far dies there
every time, with a panic that describes a facility it was never going to use.

Both ops are pointed at x86_init_noop instead. That is what Xen PV does in
xen_start_kernel() and what Hyper-V's VTL mode does in hv_vtl_init_platform(),
for the same reason and by the same two assignments -- a guest with no real
mode does not reserve a trampoline and does not initialise one.

Done where the console is wired up, which is the top of setup_arch and the
first point a cooperative guest is identifiable. The reserve call is roughly
two hundred lines further down and the init call is an initcall away, so both
are still ahead.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/setup.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/setup.c
@@ -940,6 +940,24 @@
 	 */
 	co_colinux_console_init();
 
+	/*
+	 * A cooperative guest has no real mode to trampoline through.
+	 *
+	 * reserve_real_mode() wants a page below 1 MB for the code an AP runs
+	 * on its way into long mode and for the ACPI resume stub. There are no
+	 * APs here -- the host owns the processors -- and no firmware to sleep
+	 * into, and the guest's memory begins wherever the host had blocks to
+	 * spare, which is never below 1 MB. The allocation therefore fails, and
+	 * init_real_mode() panics on the null header it is left holding.
+	 *
+	 * Xen PV and Hyper-V VTL disable both ops exactly this way, for exactly
+	 * this reason.
+	 */
+	if (co_colinux_guest) {
+		x86_platform.realmode_reserve = x86_init_noop;
+		x86_platform.realmode_init    = x86_init_noop;
+	}
+
 	idt_setup_early_traps();
 	early_cpu_init();
 	jump_label_init();
